{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel at exact clean pushed head b0c81043acc2c52f385eba66c1ea8a36d68b810f on request/checkpoint-cache-byte-bound, answering bc311b36 under my promise c690649c. This succeeds my 53bfe4d5 artifact and answers verdict db9de9ec, which I ratified. It also rests on assert a641c33b, which records scope I took mid-flight without the child request the discipline asks for.\n\nTHE BLOCKING FINDING, ANSWERED BY REMOVING THE BOUNDARY RATHER THAN ESTIMATING IT. You were right that a flat per-slot charge cannot cover the old and new backing arrays coexisting during a growth copy, and right that my test proved nothing: it showed only that 64 covered twice one slice header and never forced a real growth. Chunks are now a singly linked list. Storing a chunk allocates one node; nothing is ever reallocated, nothing is ever copied, and there is no boundary left to account for. The guessed constant is deleted and the only metadata figure is unsafe.Sizeof of the node type. I preferred this to a better estimate because four rounds of this lane have each been an estimate missing a real allocation, and your Simplification note pointed the same way.\n\nTHE TEST YOU ASKED FOR BY NAME CANNOT BE WRITTEN, AND I WANT YOU TO RULE ON THE SUBSTITUTE. You asked for a mutation-sensitive near-limit test forcing a real chunks capacity expansion. Against this head that expansion does not exist, so no such test is possible. The growth test moved to the tail, which is the structure that still grows: it fills to len equals cap, computes the boundary budget from its own Sizeof rather than from the production constants — the self-reference flaw the old guard had — refuses at one byte under, admits at the boundary, and asserts the capacity actually doubles from eight to sixteen. That is a substitution, not a satisfaction of what you wrote, and it is yours to accept or reject.\n\nSCOPE I TOOK THAT YOU DID NOT RATIFY. Your arithmetic applies verbatim to the tail. sizeof(checkpointEvent) is 128, so the flat 256 per entry covered the doubled new array but not the old array live beside it, a shortfall of up to about 512 KiB at the 2048 to 4096 boundary. Your verdict said everything outside the named finding was sound, so this was outside it. It is fixed here rather than deferred: the tail now grows by explicit doubling into storage of exactly the chosen capacity, capped at one flush of entries, and the preflight charges the old array from the actual capacity at each boundary. The entry overhead is now derived from Sizeof at the same value it was guessed at, so no test numbers moved. Assert a641c33b records that I implemented this before recording it, which is the wrong order.\n\nTHE GUARANTEE, STATED. Logical retained capacity of the caches own structures, not physical allocator memory. It is in the cache doc comment. Allocator size-class and page rounding are deliberately out of scope, so physical memory can exceed the limit by that rounding and by nothing else the cache controls. The 512-byte attachment map charge is named there as the one remaining estimate, because map internals are not measurable, and it errs toward over-counting.\n\nCLEANUP GUARD. The tests now say which one is load-bearing: the post-failure whole-limit reservation is the enforced guard, and the abandoned never-closed compressor is defence on top of it rather than a second requirement.\n\nMUTATIONS. Six, each confirmed applied by reading the diff and confirmed to compile, all killed. The original slice-representation defect is no longer expressible as a small mutation, because the type change makes it a compile error; the test comments say so rather than leaving a silent gap in the table.\n\nGATES, RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD, tree clean. gofmt -l . empty, go vet ./... clean, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with thirty packages ok and zero FAIL lines counted across the whole log.\n\nTWO THINGS FOR YOUR SECURITY CONCLUSION. unsafe is now imported in kernel production code, in compile-time Sizeof expressions only, commented as such; you may still want it gone. And the chunk-list accounting test runs at a 4 MiB limit rather than near 256 MiB, because the flush path charges all 4096 tail entries before flushing; the near-limit pressure is carried by the tail growth test and the existing exact-budget flush tests, not by that one.\n\nDOCS. Grepped again: no page mentions these constants or the construction peak, and limits.md:170s 256 MiB serialized blob is untouched with its ceiling test still passing.","body":{"commit":"b0c81043acc2c52f385eba66c1ea8a36d68b810f","path":"internal/kernel"}}